**Description:**

This Terraform configuration sets up a Route 53 record that maps a domain name to an IPv4 address. It creates a hosted zone for the domain (if it doesn't already exist) and then adds an A record pointing to the specified IPv4 address. The configuration includes a default domain name and IP address, which can be overridden by providing custom values.

**Terraform HCL:**

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

variable "domain_name" {
  description = "The domain name for which the hosted zone will be created."
  default     = "example.com"
}

variable "record_name" {
  description = "The name of the record to be created (e.g., www)."
  default     = "www"
}

variable "record_ip" {
  description = "The IPv4 address to which the record will point."
  default     = "192.0.2.1"
}

variable "ttl" {
  description = "The time-to-live (TTL) value for the record in seconds."
  default     = 300
}

resource "aws_route53_zone" "example" {
  name = var.domain_name
}

resource "aws_route53_record" "example" {
  zone_id = aws_route53_zone.example.id
  name    = var.record_name
  type    = "A"
  ttl     = var.ttl
  records = [var.record_ip]
}
```

**Notes:**
- This configuration assumes the domain name does not already exist in Route 53. If it does, the `aws_route53_zone` resource will not create a new zone.
- The `record_ip` variable uses the reserved IP address `192.0.2.1` (as per RFC 5737) for demonstration purposes. Replace it with a real IPv4 address as needed.
- The `ttl` variable defaults to 300 seconds (5 minutes), which is a common default for A records. Adjust as needed.

