**Description:**

This Terraform configuration sets up a Route 53 record that maps a domain name to an IPv6 address. It creates a hosted zone for the domain (if it doesn't already exist) and then adds an A record (IPv6) pointing to the specified IPv6 address. The configuration includes a default domain name and IPv6 address, which can be customized via variables if needed.

**Terraform HCL:**

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

variable "domain_name" {
  description = "The domain name for which the hosted zone will be created."
  default     = "example.com"
}

variable "ipv6_address" {
  description = "The IPv6 address to which the domain will be mapped."
  default     = "2001:db8::1"
}

variable "record_name" {
  description = "The subdomain or fully qualified domain name to create the record for."
  default     = "www"
}

resource "aws_route53_zone" "example" {
  name = var.domain_name
}

resource "aws_route53_record" "example" {
  zone_id = aws_route53_zone.example.id
  name    = var.record_name
  type    = "AAAA"
  ttl     = 300
  records = [var.ipv6_address]
}
```

**Notes:**
- This configuration assumes the domain does not already exist in Route 53. If it does, the `aws_route53_zone` resource will not create a new one.
- The `record_name` variable allows for specifying a subdomain (e.g., `www`) or the full domain (e.g., `example.com`).
- The `ttl` is set to 300 seconds (5 minutes) for this example, but can be adjusted as needed.
- The provider is configured for `us-east-1`, but can be changed to any valid AWS region.

