**Description:**

This Terraform configuration sets up a Route 53 hosted zone for reverse DNS (PTR) records. It creates a hosted zone named "reverse_zone" for the domain "host.example53.com" and adds a single PTR record to map an IP address to the hostname. The configuration includes the necessary AWS provider setup, IAM roles, and defaults for variables to ensure it is deployable with Terraform 1.9.8 and hashicorp/aws 5.75.

---

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

variable "reverse_zone_name" {
  description = "The name of the Route 53 hosted zone for reverse DNS."
  default     = "reverse_zone"
}

variable "domain_name" {
  description = "The domain name for the reverse DNS zone."
  default     = "host.example53.com"
}

variable "ptr_record_name" {
  description = "The name of the PTR record."
  default     = "ptr1"
}

variable "ptr_record_value" {
  description = "The IP address that the PTR record points to."
  default     = "192.168.1.10"
}

variable "ptr_record_ttl" {
  description = "The time-to-live (TTL) value for the PTR record in seconds."
  default     = 300
}

resource "aws_route53_zone" "reverse_zone" {
  name = var.domain_name
}

resource "aws_route53_record" "ptr_record" {
  zone_id = aws_route53_zone.reverse_zone.id
  name    = var.ptr_record_name
  type    = "PTR"
  ttl     = var.ptr_record_ttl
  records = [var.ptr_record_value]
}
```

---

**Usage:**

To deploy this configuration, save it to a file (e.g., `main.tf`), and run the following commands:

```bash
terraform init
terraform apply
```

This will create a Route 53 hosted zone and a PTR record for reverse DNS. You can customize the values of the variables as needed for your specific use case.

